From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: firmware timestamps around jobs

Vulkan timestamp queries (and with them GL_ARB_timer_query through zink)
need a GPU time written at a point in the command stream. This GPU has none
that a shader can read, and ROGUE_CR_TIMER is only reachable through the
register bank. The firmware can write one around a job:
ROGUE_FWIF_CCB_CMD_TYPE_PRE_TIMESTAMP and ROGUE_FWIF_CCB_CMD_TYPE_POST_TIMESTAMP
"sandwich the DM cmd" (pvr_rogue_fwif.h) and carry the firmware address to
write to. With the timer correlation of
`drm-imagination-publish-the-timer-correlation-on-monotonic-raw`
the value is a u64 in ns of CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW (measured, see there).

uAPI, kept compatible in both directions:

  - DRM_PVR_DEV_QUERY_TIMESTAMP_INFO_GET returns struct
    drm_pvr_dev_query_timestamp_info (clock, valid bits, largest buffer). It
    fails with -EOPNOTSUPP unless the GPU and firmware version are ones whose
    timestamps were measured (BXM-4-64 MC1 36.56.104.183 with firmware 1.1),
    and an older kernel fails it with -EINVAL. The value 0x100 is set apart
    from the values mainline allocates in order.
  - DRM_PVR_BO_FW_TIMESTAMPS (bit 32) creates a buffer the firmware can write
    to: at most 64 KiB each, 4 MiB together per device, not exportable, not
    with DRM_PVR_BO_PM_FW_PROTECT.
  - struct drm_pvr_job gets timestamp_flags (PRE, POST), timestamp_bo_handle
    and the two offsets. drm_pvr_job is read with copy_struct_from_user(), so
    an older userspace passes the shorter struct and gets no timestamps, and
    a newer userspace that asks for none passes zeros an older kernel accepts.

A job with timestamps is written to its CCB as

	FENCE..., PRE_TIMESTAMP, <job>, POST_TIMESTAMP, UPDATE

so the post timestamp is in memory before the job's fence signals and before
the next job on the queue starts.

The firmware writes through its own address space. The buffer is mapped
there for as long as it has a handle or a job that writes to it: each handle
and each such job holds a reference; the last one unmaps it.

Assisted-by: Claude
